const DATA={"summary": [{"title": "1. From understanding architecture to changing it", "slides": "1–5", "lead": "Lecture 3 explains how layers fit together; Lecture 4 explains how to move an organisation from what exists now toward an agreed improved state.", "blocks": [["Running university case", "The registration system crosses four layers: <strong>business<\/strong> registration rules and responsibilities; <strong>data<\/strong> for student, course, fee and enrolment; <strong>applications<\/strong> such as portal, student system and finance; and <strong>technology<\/strong> such as hosting, network, identity and security (slide 2). Changes must work across all four."], ["Learning outcomes", "The lecture asks students to explain <strong>baseline and target-state architecture<\/strong>, use <strong>gap analysis<\/strong> to identify changes and dependencies, construct a roadmap, and explain <strong>governance and decision rights<\/strong> while applying a simplified TOGAF ADM approach (slide 4)."], ["Start with outcomes, not a purchase", "“Buy a new system” is a proposed solution, not a target state. A proper target describes the desired ability or service, such as <strong>one reliable enrolment status across systems<\/strong>, easier confirmation for students and trusted information for staff and management. Define the desired improvement first, then explore which technologies can enable it."]]}, {"title": "2. Baseline architecture versus target-state architecture", "slides": "5–9", "lead": "The baseline is the current way the organisation works; the target is its agreed future way of working, constrained by goals and resources.", "blocks": [["Baseline=current state", "The <strong>baseline architecture<\/strong> documents how business processes, data, applications and technology operate today. For registration, it may reveal multiple status definitions, late updates and repeated manual corrections. A useful baseline uses evidence rather than assumptions, because comparison to the target depends on understanding the real starting point."], ["Target=desired capabilities", "The <strong>target-state architecture<\/strong> describes how the organisation should work after improvements. Slide 7 gives business ownership and simpler registration steps, agreed data definitions and authoritative student records, reliable portal/core-system exchange, and resilient, secure and scalable technology. It is not a “perfect wish list”; it must address goals and constraints."], ["Success measures before design", "The slide suggests outcomes for <strong>students<\/strong> (confirm enrolment without repeated visits), <strong>staff<\/strong> (fewer duplicates and corrections), <strong>management<\/strong> (trustworthy timely reporting), and <strong>service<\/strong> (reliable access during busy registration). Set a current baseline and a future target for each outcome instead of relying on vague promises."], ["Example target-state flow", "Slide 9 shows <strong>one portal → checks authoritative student/course data → finance status exchanged through controlled integration → shared status visible to authorised users<\/strong>. Monitoring, access controls, backup and support are designed into the solution."]]}, {"title": "3. Architecture roadmaps: a staged route to the target", "slides": "10–15", "lead": "A roadmap converts a desired future into a realistic sequence of initiatives, owners, milestones and dependencies.", "blocks": [["Roadmap definition", "The lecture describes a roadmap as a <strong>sequenced plan for moving from baseline to target<\/strong>. It should show initiatives, timing, dependencies and milestones while connecting technical work to business value. It must include people and process change as well as data, applications and technology, not merely a list of coding tasks."], ["Five important dependencies", "Slide 12 stresses: <strong>(1)<\/strong> agree data ownership before automating exchange; <strong>(2)<\/strong> clean critical records before migration; <strong>(3)<\/strong> design integration before connecting production; <strong>(4)<\/strong> test security, load and recovery before peak registration; <strong>(5)<\/strong> prepare student/staff support before launch. The ordering matters because later tasks rely on earlier decisions."], ["Three-stage illustrative roadmap", "An example based on the lecture’s activity solution is <strong>Stage 1: Agree processes, data rules and a baseline;<\/strong> <strong>Stage 2: design and pilot controlled integration;<\/strong> <strong>Stage 3: phased rollout with testing, support and monitoring.<\/strong> Each stage should have an accountable owner and evidence of completion. The sequence is a worked example, not a new mandatory framework from the lecture."], ["Roadmap quality check", "Ask whether each initiative has an outcome, a predecessor, an owner and a way of determining when it is ready. A roadmap without dependencies may schedule data migration before data rules are agreed, creating predictable rework."]]}, {"title": "4. Gap analysis as the bridge between baseline and target", "slides": "16–18", "lead": "Gap analysis identifies missing capabilities and required changes, not merely a list of software products to buy.", "blocks": [["Baseline → Gap → Target", "Compare what exists today with the agreed target to identify what is absent or inadequate. Classify gaps as <strong>business, data, application, technology, people or governance<\/strong>. Record the evidence, impact, priority and possible action (slide 16). A gap should become actionable work rather than a vague complaint."], ["Official registration gap example", "<strong>Current:<\/strong> systems store different enrolment status values. <strong>Target:<\/strong> an agreed status with a defined source of truth. <strong>Gap:<\/strong> data definitions, ownership and integration rules are missing. <strong>Action:<\/strong> agree meaning and ownership, design exchange and test reconciliation. <strong>Dependency:<\/strong> resolve data rules before full automation (slide 17)."], ["Look beyond technology", "Examples on slide 18 include unclear <strong>business<\/strong> process ownership; duplicate/inconsistent <strong>data<\/strong>; no reliable <strong>application<\/strong> exchange; weak <strong>technology<\/strong> monitoring/recovery; and missing <strong>governance<\/strong> approval routes for exceptions. A new system alone cannot resolve all five categories."], ["Turning gaps into priorities", "High-impact gaps that block other work should be addressed early. For example, an authoritative enrolment definition is foundational to successful data exchange, whereas a minor interface enhancement may wait until core information is reliable. Explain each priority with the dependency or business risk it addresses."]]}, {"title": "5. Architecture governance: principles, decisions, exceptions", "slides": "19–22", "lead": "Governance gives the organisation a clear and accountable way to judge proposed architecture changes.", "blocks": [["What governance establishes", "Governance sets <strong>principles, decision rights and review points<\/strong>. It checks proposals against the agreed architecture, balances business value, cost, risk, security and standards, and records exceptions and who accepted the associated risk (slide 19). It should help good decisions rather than generate unnecessary paperwork."], ["Principles as decision rules", "Examples include “<strong>authoritative student data has a named owner<\/strong>” and “<strong>integrate through approved, documented interfaces<\/strong>” (slide 21). Principles make expectations explicit so different teams do not invent incompatible approaches. They also help reviewers explain why a choice is accepted or challenged."], ["Architecture review", "The lecture’s review dimensions are <strong>business fit, data, integration, security, operations and cost<\/strong>. A proposal may be <strong>approved<\/strong>, sent back to be <strong>revised<\/strong>, or allowed as an <strong>approved documented exception<\/strong>. An exception is not silent non-compliance: the responsible authority records why it is acceptable and who carries the risk."], ["Decision rights and ownership", "An essay should name an appropriate accountable owner for each decision, such as a business/process owner for enrolment policy, a designated data owner for status definitions, and architecture/security authorities for interfaces and access controls. These role assignments are illustrative applications of the lecture’s governance concept; the slides do not prescribe the university’s job titles."]]}, {"title": "6. TOGAF ADM as a structured but adaptable method", "slides": "23–25", "lead": "TOGAF is an Enterprise Architecture standard/framework, and ADM is its central development method. The lecture uses a simplified practical view.", "blocks": [["Why ADM matters", "ADM helps organise work from an architecture vision through design, implementation and change. It is <strong>iterative<\/strong>, meaning teams can revisit activities as they learn. The lecture explicitly advises adapting the method to the initiative’s scope, scale, risk and organisational capacity rather than following it mechanically."], ["Six-step simplified university application", "Slide 25 applies: <strong>1 Vision:<\/strong> reduce friction and improve record consistency; <strong>2 Architecture:<\/strong> agree processes, ownership, interfaces and platforms; <strong>3 Solutions:<\/strong> compare options and sequence a pilot; <strong>4 Migration:<\/strong> plan stages, dependencies and cutover; <strong>5 Governance:<\/strong> review delivery against approved architecture; <strong>6 Change:<\/strong> assess new requirements and update the roadmap."], ["Iteration, not linear box-ticking", "If integration tests reveal unclear enrolment meanings, the team may return to data-architecture decisions before rolling out. That is consistent with an iterative method. An exam answer should explain this feedback loop and avoid treating the six-step lecture simplification as the complete official TOGAF phase catalogue."], ["Connection to other work products", "Architecture vision, baseline/target views, gap analysis, roadmap and governance records create evidence for different parts of the method. The aim is to guide decisions through change, not to maximise the number of documents."]]}, {"title": "7. Work products and a traceable change story", "slides": "25–27", "lead": "Useful architecture artefacts provide a connected explanation from problem to governed implementation.", "blocks": [["Architecture vision", "Describes the <strong>problem, scope, stakeholders and desired outcomes<\/strong>. For the university, the vision may aim to make registration less confusing and to reduce inconsistent records across departments. It should be understood before choosing a vendor or beginning code."], ["Baseline/target views and gap analysis", "The baseline/target comparison explains what works today and what is intended for tomorrow. Gap analysis describes the missing capabilities and changes needed between those states. The two artefacts must use the same scope so the roadmap addresses real differences rather than unrelated ideas."], ["Roadmap and governance record", "The roadmap identifies initiatives, order, owners and milestones. A governance record captures decisions, justified exceptions and follow-up. Together they provide accountability: readers can see not only what is proposed but why, when, by whom and under which constraints."], ["Avoid six mistakes", "The lecture warns against choosing products before targets, unrealistic “perfect” targets, ignoring data/process changes, roadmaps without dependencies or owners, governance as a final sign-off only, and mechanical framework use. All six are practical structured-question discussion points."]]}, {"title": "8. Complete university registration worked example", "slides": "5–9, 12, 17, 21, 25, 28–29", "lead": "Use the same case across target state, gap analysis, roadmap, governance and simplified ADM.", "blocks": [["Target state across four layers", "Business: simpler registration steps with clear ownership. Data: trusted student and enrolment status definitions. Applications: portal, records and finance exchange approved information. Technology: monitored, secure and resilient access through peak periods. The student should see one trustworthy registration status."], ["Three critical gaps", "<strong>Gap 1:<\/strong> no agreed enrolment-status owner/meaning. <strong>Gap 2:<\/strong> inconsistent records across systems. <strong>Gap 3:<\/strong> untested exchange and weak recovery under failures. All affect the target and should be prioritised based on their impact and dependencies."], ["Stage and govern the change", "<strong>Stage 1:<\/strong> agree rules, owners and current measurements. <strong>Stage 2:<\/strong> clean data, design and pilot integration. <strong>Stage 3:<\/strong> validate security, load and recovery; train users; roll out in phases and monitor. Governance decisions include approval of authoritative definitions and approval of the interface/security design. These sample decisions are explicitly consistent with slide 29’s activity solution."], ["Measure outcomes", "Check registration completion time, error rate, failed transactions and support request volume, comparing results with a measured baseline. When outcomes fall short, revisit gaps, designs, rollout assumptions and the roadmap instead of declaring success merely because a platform went live."]]}, {"title": "9. Cross-topic comparisons that score explanation marks", "slides": "6–7, 11–12, 16–21", "lead": "Many structured questions ask you to compare related concepts and say how they work together.", "blocks": [["Target state versus roadmap", "<strong>Target state<\/strong> describes where the enterprise intends to arrive. A <strong>roadmap<\/strong> explains how to get there in stages. A target without a plan may remain only an aspiration; a roadmap without a target may deliver tasks that do not improve the organisation."], ["Gap versus risk versus dependency", "A <strong>gap<\/strong> is the difference between baseline and target. A <strong>risk<\/strong> is a possible event or condition that could cause harm, such as migration failure. A <strong>dependency<\/strong> is the order or relationship among tasks, such as agreeing status definitions before enabling full exchange. These concepts interact but are not interchangeable."], ["Governance versus implementation", "Implementation produces the change; governance ensures important decisions and deviations are reviewed by accountable people. A governance decision may stop an unsafe design or require revision without performing the coding itself. Governance should continue during delivery, not appear only after launch."], ["Vision versus solution", "The vision expresses a desired capability or outcome, such as reliable registration and reporting. A new portal is only one possible solution. Alternatives must be judged against the intended capability, constraints and other systems."]]}, {"title": "10. Exam writing checklist and official takeaways", "slides": "24–30", "lead": "The lecture is primarily about applying architectural reasoning to a realistic university case.", "blocks": [["Five official takeaways", "<strong>1.<\/strong> Target state defines the intended future, not merely a system. <strong>2.<\/strong> Roadmaps sequence change around value, dependencies and risk. <strong>3.<\/strong> Gap analysis turns differences into actionable work. <strong>4.<\/strong> Governance clarifies principles, decisions and exceptions. <strong>5.<\/strong> TOGAF ADM provides a structured but adaptable path from vision to governed change (slide 30)."], ["Template for 10–12-mark answers", "Use <strong>desired business outcome → current baseline → target across four layers → three evidence-based gaps → staged roadmap with dependencies → governance owners and decisions → success measures<\/strong>. For TOGAF questions, show how an iterative vision/architecture/solutions/migration/governance/change flow supports the work."], ["Acknowledge assumptions", "If the question does not provide actual numbers or departmental ownership, describe suggested measures and proposed owners rather than claiming that the university already uses them. The scenario’s worked steps are an application of the lecture, not official implementation facts."]]}], "essays": [{"topic": "Targets", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 8, "slides": "5–7", "question": "Differentiate baseline architecture and target-state architecture.", "points": ["Baseline=current operation", "Target=agreed future", "Both cover four layers", "Business constraints"], "answer": "Baseline architecture describes how the organisation currently operates, including its business processes, data, applications and technology. It provides evidence about existing problems and dependencies. Target-state architecture describes the agreed future way of working that can deliver desired business outcomes within practical constraints. For a university, the baseline may involve conflicting enrolment statuses across separate systems, while the target has an agreed source of truth and reliable exchange. Both views cover the same four architectural layers so they can be compared. Defining the target does not mean choosing a new product immediately; it means describing the capability the university needs to achieve."}, {"topic": "Targets", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 10, "slides": "5–9", "question": "Explain why “buy a new registration system” is not a valid target-state description by itself.", "points": ["Solution versus capability", "Stakeholder outcomes", "Four EA layers", "Measures"], "answer": "Buying a system describes a potential solution, not the future capability the university wants. A new portal could be purchased while student records, finance status and reporting remain inconsistent. A useful target should first specify outcomes, such as fewer repeated visits, clear enrolment information, less manual correction and reliable access during peak demand. It should then describe the necessary business ownership, data definitions, application integration and technology foundations. Only after the future state is understood should the university compare technology options. This prevents procurement from replacing architectural analysis and provides measurable criteria for deciding whether the investment delivered value."}, {"topic": "Targets", "kind": "Apply", "marks": 12, "slides": "7–9", "question": "Propose a target state for online university registration using the four EA layers.", "points": ["Business ownership/process", "Data authoritative source", "Applications/interface", "Technology qualities", "Student outcome"], "answer": "At the business layer, the university should establish clear registration responsibilities and a simpler process for students and staff. At the data layer, it should agree on definitions and trusted sources for student, course and enrolment status. The application layer should allow the portal and core systems, including student records and finance, to exchange approved information so authorised users see one reliable status. The technology layer should provide monitoring, secure access, backup and enough capacity for busy registration periods. The resulting target state is not simply a new website; it is a coherent student service that reduces confusion and manual reconciliation. Success can be evaluated through completion time, errors and support demand."}, {"topic": "Targets", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 10, "slides": "8–9", "question": "Explain why success measures should be defined before target-state design.", "points": ["Stakeholder outcomes", "Baseline/target", "Guides architecture", "Examples and feedback"], "answer": "Success measures clarify what the organisation intends to improve and help prevent technology choices from becoming ends in themselves. Before designing registration, the university should record current student waiting or completion time, staff correction workload, reliability of reporting and failed transactions during peaks. It can then set realistic targets for improvement. The architecture can be designed around those outcomes, including better data sharing and infrastructure capacity. After rollout, the same definitions and measures can be used to evaluate whether the new service created the expected value. Without early measurement, a visually improved portal could be declared successful even when organisational problems remain unchanged."}, {"topic": "Roadmaps", "kind": "Define", "marks": 8, "slides": "11", "question": "Define an architecture roadmap and describe its essential components.", "points": ["Sequence baseline to target", "Initiatives/timing/milestones", "Dependencies/owners", "Business and technical change"], "answer": "An architecture roadmap is a sequenced plan that moves an organisation from its baseline state toward an agreed target state. It identifies the initiatives needed, their order, timing, dependencies and milestones, including responsibility for delivery. A complete roadmap covers business processes and people as well as data, applications and technology. For example, a registration roadmap might begin by agreeing data ownership, continue with integration testing and finish with a phased launch and student support. Its value is that it connects technical tasks to organisational outcomes and reduces avoidable risk from implementing dependent changes in the wrong order."}, {"topic": "Roadmaps", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 10, "slides": "12", "question": "Explain five dependencies that should influence the university registration roadmap.", "points": ["Data ownership before exchange", "Clean records before migration", "Design integration before production", "Test before peak demand", "Support before launch"], "answer": "The university should first agree on who owns important data, because automated exchange cannot resolve conflicting definitions. It should then clean critical student records before migration to avoid transferring known errors into the new service. Third, the team must design the integration between registration, finance and student records before connecting production systems. Fourth, it should test security, peak load and recovery before the busiest registration period, when a failure would affect many students. Finally, staff and student support must be prepared before launch so users can complete tasks and resolve problems. These dependencies explain why a roadmap must be sequenced rather than a list of unrelated deliverables."}, {"topic": "Roadmaps", "kind": "Design", "marks": 12, "slides": "11–12, 29", "question": "Create a justified three-stage roadmap for improving university course registration.", "points": ["Stage 1 baseline/rules", "Stage 2 pilot/integration", "Stage 3 phased launch", "Dependencies", "Owners and success"], "answer": "In Stage 1, the university should document the current registration problems, identify business and data owners, agree data definitions and collect performance baselines. This prepares a reliable foundation for technical work. In Stage 2, the team should clean critical records, design approved interfaces and run a bounded integration pilot between the portal, student records and finance. This allows important problems to be detected before wide deployment. In Stage 3, it should test load, security and recovery, train staff, prepare student support and roll out in phases. Each stage should have an accountable owner and milestone. The order is justified because data rules and tested interfaces are prerequisites to dependable full automation."}, {"topic": "Roadmaps", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 10, "slides": "11–12, 27", "question": "A roadmap schedules full integration before data ownership is agreed. Analyse why this is risky.", "points": ["Wrong dependency order", "Inconsistent definitions", "Rework and errors", "Recommended rescheduling"], "answer": "The roadmap violates an important dependency: the organisation needs an agreed meaning and owner for shared enrolment status before it can automate exchange reliably. If teams connect systems that interpret the same field differently, they may distribute incorrect information more quickly. Late discovery of the inconsistency can cause interface redesign, rework, delayed delivery and loss of trust in reports. I would revise the roadmap so the process and data owners first agree definitions, authoritative sources and reconciliation rules. Integration design and pilot testing should follow, with production rollout only after the exchanged information is proven consistent. This shows why sequencing is an architecture decision, not merely a scheduling preference."}, {"topic": "Roadmaps", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 8, "slides": "6, 11", "question": "Differentiate target-state architecture from an architecture roadmap.", "points": ["Target=destination", "Roadmap=journey", "Dependencies", "Example"], "answer": "Target-state architecture describes how the organisation intends to work in the future. For registration, it may specify a single trustworthy enrolment status, consistent data meanings, integrated applications and reliable infrastructure. An architecture roadmap describes how to reach that target through timed and sequenced initiatives. It might first clarify data ownership, then test integration and finally roll out the connected service. The target provides a common destination against which options are judged; the roadmap provides an executable route that recognises dependencies, responsibilities and risk. The two are related but cannot replace one another."}, {"topic": "Gaps", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 10, "slides": "16–18", "question": "Define gap analysis and explain how to make it actionable.", "points": ["Current/target comparison", "Missing capabilities", "Categories", "Evidence/priority/action", "Dependency"], "answer": "Gap analysis compares the baseline architecture with the target state to identify missing or inadequate capabilities. The lecture advises examining business, data, application, technology, people and governance gaps rather than listing software products alone. Each gap should be supported by evidence and described in terms of its impact and priority. An action is then proposed, with any dependencies needed before implementation. For example, if two systems show different enrolment statuses, the gap concerns the shared definition and ownership of the status as well as integration rules. Agreeing those rules becomes an actionable prerequisite to full automation."}, {"topic": "Gaps", "kind": "Apply", "marks": 12, "slides": "17", "question": "Reconstruct the lecture’s enrolment-status gap analysis from current state to action and dependency.", "points": ["Current inconsistent status", "Target source of truth", "Missing definitions/owners/rules", "Actions", "Dependency order"], "answer": "In the current state, the university’s separate systems store different enrolment statuses, so staff and students cannot always rely on a consistent answer. The target state is one agreed status with a defined source of truth. The gaps include missing status definitions, unclear data ownership and absent integration rules. The university should therefore agree what each status means, name the authoritative owner, design the required exchange and test how records are reconciled across systems. The critical dependency is to settle the data rules before enabling full automation. Otherwise, connecting the systems may only make inconsistent information spread faster."}, {"topic": "Gaps", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 10, "slides": "16–18", "question": "Give one concrete example of each of five non-identical architecture gap categories.", "points": ["Business", "Data", "Application", "Technology", "Governance"], "answer": "A business gap occurs when nobody clearly owns the process for handling registration exceptions. A data gap exists when student status is duplicated or has different meanings in separate systems. An application gap occurs when the registration portal cannot exchange updates reliably with finance. A technology gap arises when monitoring or recovery capabilities are too weak for a critical service. A governance gap exists when the organisation has no route for reviewing or approving architecture exceptions. Each category needs a different type of action. Buying a new portal alone will not resolve missing process ownership or absent governance rules, which explains why gap analysis must look beyond software components."}, {"topic": "Gaps", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 10, "slides": "16–18", "question": "Explain why gap analysis should include people and governance as well as technical gaps.", "points": ["Adoption requires people", "Process ownership", "Decision rights", "Technical-only fixes inadequate"], "answer": "Technology is implemented within an organisation, so its success depends on people making decisions and operating changed processes. Even after a registration portal is integrated, staff may lack the training or authority to resolve an inconsistent enrolment status. Similarly, without governance, teams may use unapproved interfaces or ignore agreed data standards. Gap analysis should therefore identify missing skills, responsibilities, approval routes and support arrangements alongside data, application and infrastructure gaps. This creates an actionable change plan that addresses the whole service, not merely its software installation. A technically correct platform can still fail organisationally if these non-technical capabilities are missing."}, {"topic": "Gaps", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 10, "slides": "16–18", "question": "An architecture team reports only “we need a new database” as its gap analysis. Evaluate the adequacy.", "points": ["Product is not a gap definition", "Need evidence of current/target", "Ownership/definition", "Alternative actions", "Rewrite a proper gap"], "answer": "The analysis is inadequate because “need a new database” is already a proposed solution and does not explain what the organisation cannot do. A useful gap analysis should compare the baseline and target and identify the missing capability, supported by evidence. For example, the actual gap may be inconsistent student-status definitions, poor data ownership or unreliable exchange between applications. A database replacement may not fix any of those issues. I would first describe the observed inconsistency, its impact and the agreed target, then compare actions such as better data definitions, reconciliation controls and approved integration. Only if the evidence shows the database itself is the constraint would replacement become a justified roadmap initiative."}, {"topic": "Governance", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 10, "slides": "19–21", "question": "Explain architecture governance and why decision rights matter.", "points": ["Principles", "Decision rights", "Architecture review", "Risk/exception records", "Purpose"], "answer": "Architecture governance is the arrangement of principles, decision rights and review points used to guide important architectural changes. It ensures proposals are checked against business value, cost, security, standards and the agreed architecture. Decision rights clarify who is authorised to approve a data definition, integration design or risk exception. This reduces confusion and prevents teams from silently making incompatible local decisions. An exception can be accepted when properly justified, but it should be documented together with the responsible person and associated risk. The lecture stresses that governance should enable good decisions rather than create paperwork for its own sake."}, {"topic": "Governance", "kind": "Apply", "marks": 8, "slides": "21", "question": "Describe two example architecture principles for a university and their practical effects.", "points": ["Named data owner", "Approved documented interfaces", "Consistent decisions", "Review implications"], "answer": "One principle is that every authoritative student-data item has a named owner. This means the university must identify who defines, validates and approves changes to information such as enrolment status. A second principle is that systems should integrate through approved and documented interfaces. This reduces undocumented connections and helps teams understand how information flows between student records and finance. Together the principles establish expectations before different departments develop solutions. Reviewers can use them to decide whether a proposal should be approved, revised or handled through a documented exception."}, {"topic": "Governance", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 10, "slides": "19–21", "question": "A faculty wants to bypass an approved interface for a quick deadline. Analyse the governance decision.", "points": ["Business benefit", "Integration/security risk", "Principles", "Decision options", "Accountability"], "answer": "The faculty may have a legitimate need to deliver quickly, but bypassing an approved interface can create security, maintenance and data-consistency risks. Architecture governance should review the proposal using business fit, data, integration, security, operations and cost. If it can meet the need using the approved approach, the team should revise its plan accordingly. If a temporary alternative is justified, the appropriate authority may approve a documented exception, record who accepts the risk and define any follow-up conditions. This protects accountability while allowing proportionate flexibility. Silently ignoring the principle is not equivalent to a governed exception."}, {"topic": "Governance", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 10, "slides": "19–21", "question": "Distinguish approval, revision and a documented architecture exception.", "points": ["Approval", "Revision", "Exception", "Risk owner", "Practical scenario"], "answer": "Approval means the architecture proposal satisfies the agreed principles and review requirements sufficiently to proceed. Revision means the proposal has important deficiencies that must be corrected, for example missing evidence of secure integration. A documented exception permits a justified departure from a principle, provided an authorised decision-maker accepts and records the resulting risk and follow-up requirements. For a registration platform, a standard interface design could be approved, while an insecure direct database connection might require revision. A temporary deviation might be considered only with clear justification and accountability. These alternatives show that governance is a decision process, not merely a yes-or-no form."}, {"topic": "Governance", "kind": "Design", "marks": 12, "slides": "19–21, 28–29", "question": "Propose two architecture governance decisions and appropriate owners for the registration project.", "points": ["Data definition approval", "Interface/security design approval", "Proposed owner roles", "Decision criteria and records"], "answer": "The first decision is to approve the definition and authoritative source of enrolment status. A designated university data owner, working with the responsible student administration and finance process owners, should be accountable for agreeing what the status means and who may update it. The second decision is to approve the interface and security design connecting the portal, student records and finance. An appointed architecture or integration authority, supported by security specialists, should review the design for compatibility, access controls and operational reliability. Both decisions should be recorded with responsible owners and any conditions or exceptions. These are illustrative role assignments applying the lecture’s governance requirements rather than official university posts."}, {"topic": "ADM", "kind": "Define", "marks": 8, "slides": "23", "question": "Explain TOGAF ADM’s role in architecture work and the meaning of iteration.", "points": ["TOGAF framework", "ADM central method", "Vision to change", "Revisiting phases", "Tailoring"], "answer": "TOGAF is an Enterprise Architecture framework or standard, and ADM is its central method for organising architecture development. It helps teams move from a vision through architecture decisions and implementation toward ongoing change. ADM is iterative: work may be revisited when new requirements, constraints or evidence appear. For example, an integration pilot may reveal that student-status definitions are unclear, requiring the team to reconsider data architecture before deployment. The lecture also advises tailoring the method to project scope, risk, scale and organisational capacity. The simplified steps taught in the lecture are a practical teaching view rather than a substitute for every formal ADM detail."}, {"topic": "ADM", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 12, "slides": "25", "question": "Explain the six simplified TOGAF ADM activities using the university registration case.", "points": ["Vision", "Architecture", "Solutions", "Migration", "Governance", "Change"], "answer": "First, Vision defines the intended value, such as reducing registration friction and improving record consistency. Second, Architecture agrees the business process, data ownership, required interfaces and platform capabilities. Third, Solutions compares possible approaches and plans a pilot. Fourth, Migration sequences dependencies, rollout stages and cutover. Fifth, Governance checks whether delivery follows approved principles and designs, recording decisions or exceptions where needed. Sixth, Change assesses new requirements or findings and updates the roadmap. These activities form an iterative approach: if a pilot reveals new problems, the team can revisit earlier architectural decisions. Thus, ADM provides a structured but adaptable route from outcomes to governed change."}, {"topic": "ADM", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 10, "slides": "23–25", "question": "Explain why a team should not treat simplified TOGAF ADM as a rigid one-way checklist.", "points": ["Iterative lifecycle", "Feedback from pilots", "Tailoring to scope", "Revised designs/roadmap", "Value"], "answer": "The lecture describes ADM as iterative and adaptable, not a fixed sequence that can never be revisited. In the university case, a pilot might reveal that the finance system uses a different meaning for payment confirmation. Continuing straight to rollout would create predictable inconsistency. Instead, the team should revisit data definitions and integration design, then adjust the migration plan. The method should also be tailored to the project’s size, risks and available capacity, avoiding excessive process for a small, bounded change. The purpose is to improve architectural decisions while responding to evidence, not to satisfy a checklist regardless of what the organisation learns."}, {"topic": "ADM", "kind": "Apply", "marks": 10, "slides": "23–26", "question": "Show how a gap discovered during a pilot can change the ADM-based roadmap.", "points": ["Pilot observation", "Return to architecture", "Revise solution", "Update migration", "Governance review"], "answer": "Suppose an integration pilot reveals that a payment status reaches the portal several hours after finance confirms it. The team should first record the issue and assess its effect on the target outcome of reliable enrolment status. It would then revisit the data and application architecture to clarify the source, meaning and timing of updates. The solution design may need an improved interface or reconciliation mechanism. Migration should be rescheduled so the issue is tested before wider rollout. Governance should review and approve the changed approach. This demonstrates the iterative relationship between architecture, solutions, deployment plans and ongoing change."}, {"topic": "ADM", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 8, "slides": "23–26", "question": "Explain how an architecture vision and a governance record support different stages of architecture-led change.", "points": ["Vision problem/goal/stakeholders", "Governance decisions/exceptions", "Different functions", "Connection through implementation"], "answer": "An architecture vision describes the problem, scope, stakeholders and intended organisational outcomes at the beginning of the work. In registration, it might establish the goal of reliable status information and fewer manual corrections. A governance record captures important decisions, approved approaches, justified exceptions and follow-up during development and rollout. The vision helps everyone understand why the change is needed; the governance record shows how decisions were controlled as that vision was implemented. They are complementary. Without a vision, decisions can lack direction; without governance, the chosen direction can be undermined by inconsistent local changes."}, {"topic": "Cases", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 12, "slides": "5–29", "question": "Using the running case, propose a target, three gaps, and a three-stage roadmap.", "points": ["Four-layer target", "Three distinct gaps", "Three stages", "Dependencies", "Governance/metrics"], "answer": "The target state is a student-centred registration service with clear business ownership, trusted student data, approved links between the portal and core systems, and reliable secure infrastructure. Three gaps are unclear ownership of enrolment status, inconsistent records across departments and untested integration or recovery behaviour. Stage 1 should clarify processes, data meanings and current performance. Stage 2 should clean important records, design integration and pilot the connection. Stage 3 should test security, load and recovery, train staff, provide student support and roll out in phases. The order is essential because agreed data rules precede dependable automation. Governance should approve definitions and interface designs, while success should be measured through errors, completion time and failed transactions."}, {"topic": "Cases", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 12, "slides": "6–29", "question": "Evaluate a university roadmap that has milestones but no owners, dependencies or success measures.", "points": ["Incomplete roadmap", "Unclear accountability", "Wrong order risk", "No outcome evidence", "Improved roadmap"], "answer": "The roadmap is incomplete because milestones alone do not show who is responsible, what must happen first or how success will be judged. If data migration is scheduled before authoritative data definitions are agreed, the university may transfer inconsistent records into the new service. Without owners, teams may delay decisions or assume another department is responsible. Without success measures, the project may claim success simply because the portal launches, even if students still face delays. I would add accountable owners, dependency links, stage acceptance checks and a baseline and target for service outcomes. This turns a calendar of tasks into a credible architecture roadmap."}, {"topic": "Cases", "kind": "Discuss", "marks": 12, "slides": "5–30", "question": "Discuss how baseline/target views, gap analysis, roadmap and governance work together.", "points": ["Baseline", "Target", "Gaps", "Roadmap", "Governance and feedback"], "answer": "Baseline architecture describes current processes, information, applications and infrastructure, establishing the starting point. Target-state architecture describes the agreed future capability based on organisational goals. Gap analysis then identifies the differences and converts them into actions, including missing data ownership, integration and recovery. The roadmap sequences these actions according to value, dependencies and risk, assigning milestones and owners. Governance checks important proposals against principles, reviews exceptions and records accountability throughout implementation. Together these work products create a traceable change story. If a pilot reveals new evidence, the target, gaps or roadmap can be revisited through the iterative architecture process."}, {"topic": "Cases", "kind": "Apply", "marks": 10, "slides": "8, 17, 29", "question": "Propose four practical performance indicators for the university registration improvement.", "points": ["Completion time", "Error/duplicate rate", "Failed transaction rate", "Support volume", "Baseline/target"], "answer": "Registration completion time measures how efficiently students can finish the process. The error or duplicate-entry rate measures whether data definitions and integration have improved information consistency. Failed transactions indicate how reliably the portal and related systems operate, especially during peak demand. The volume of helpdesk or student-support requests can show whether the service is easier to use and manage. The university should record a baseline before the change, agree realistic targets and use the same definitions during evaluation. These indicators connect the architecture work to outcomes that matter for students, staff and management instead of measuring only whether software was installed."}, {"topic": "Cases", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 10, "slides": "7–8, 18, 27", "question": "Explain why people, policy and support must be part of a target-state architecture.", "points": ["Technology alone insufficient", "Role clarity", "Policy/process", "Training/support", "Sustainable adoption"], "answer": "A target state describes how the organisation is intended to operate, not just what software it will own. Registration policies may need clarification so students receive consistent decisions, while process ownership determines who handles disputes or unusual situations. Staff need training to operate new interfaces and reconcile exceptional records, and students need support during the transition. Without these changes, a technically integrated portal may still create confusion or produce inconsistent service. Therefore, people, policy and support should be designed alongside the four architectural layers. This reflects the lecture’s warning not to ignore business process change when planning a new target."}, {"topic": "Recap", "kind": "Explain", "marks": 10, "slides": "27, 30", "question": "Explain the five official takeaways of the lecture and how they relate to one another.", "points": ["Target future", "Roadmap sequencing", "Gap analysis actions", "Governance choices", "ADM structured adaptable"], "answer": "The first takeaway is that the target state describes a desired organisational future rather than just a new system. Second, a roadmap sequences the transition according to value, dependencies and risk. Third, gap analysis compares the current and desired states and identifies the actions required to bridge them. Fourth, governance establishes principles, decision rights and documented exceptions so changes remain accountable. Fifth, TOGAF ADM provides a structured but adaptable method for moving from a vision to governed change. These ideas work as one process: define the future, understand what is missing, plan the work, make controlled decisions and revisit the approach as new evidence appears."}, {"topic": "Recap", "kind": "Evaluate", "marks": 10, "slides": "27–30", "question": "A manager says “We followed TOGAF, so our registration project must be successful.” Evaluate the claim.", "points": ["Method not guarantee", "Target outcomes", "Evidence", "Risks/feedback", "Governance and iteration"], "answer": "Using TOGAF or ADM does not guarantee success because a framework structures work rather than automatically solving an organisation’s problems. A project may follow formal activities yet choose an unrealistic target, ignore data ownership or fail to measure student outcomes. The university must still determine whether registration became more reliable, whether manual corrections decreased and whether peak access works. Governance should review important decisions and exceptions, while feedback should lead to changes when the intended value is not achieved. The lecture explicitly advises tailoring ADM instead of following it mechanically. Success depends on the quality of organisational decisions and demonstrated results, not merely the name of the method used."}, {"topic": "Recap", "kind": "Apply", "marks": 10, "slides": "6–30", "question": "Outline a complete answer structure for a structured exam question about EA transformation.", "points": ["Goal/target", "Current baseline", "Four-layer view", "Gaps/roadmap", "Governance/value"], "answer": "I would begin by stating the university’s business goal and describing the desired registration capability, rather than immediately proposing a platform. Next, I would summarise the baseline and target across business, data, application and technology architecture. I would identify evidence-based gaps and explain their impact. I would then propose a staged roadmap that respects dependencies, especially the need to agree data ownership before automation. Finally, I would name suitable governance decisions and responsible owners, and explain how completion time, error rates and support requests could measure the results. This structure demonstrates both conceptual understanding and practical application to the case."}, {"topic": "Recap", "kind": "Compare", "marks": 8, "slides": "16–21", "question": "Differentiate an architecture gap, an implementation dependency and a governance exception.", "points": ["Define gap", "Define dependency", "Define exception", "Example"], "answer": "An architecture gap is the difference between the current and desired capability, such as missing authoritative enrolment data. An implementation dependency is a required order of work, such as agreeing the data definition before configuring automated exchange. A governance exception is a formally authorised and documented departure from an architectural principle or standard, with a named person accepting the related risk. These concepts describe different aspects of architecture-led change: what is missing, what must happen first and how deviations are controlled. Distinguishing them prevents a team from treating a schedule problem or an unapproved shortcut as if it were simply another feature request."}, {"topic": "Recap", "kind": "Analyse", "marks": 10, "slides": "19–30", "question": "Why must governance continue after a solution is approved?", "points": ["Changing requirements", "Delivery deviations", "Security/operations risks", "Exceptions and accountability", "Feedback"], "answer": "Initial approval only checks the proposed direction against information available at that moment. During implementation, integration tests may reveal incompatible data, security concerns or operational limitations. Staff requirements and business priorities may also change. Ongoing governance checks whether delivery remains consistent with agreed principles, and ensures that revisions or exceptions are reviewed by accountable owners. It also connects findings from testing and rollout with updates to the roadmap. If governance stops at sign-off, teams may introduce undocumented shortcuts or overlook new risks. Therefore, architecture governance should enable responsible decisions across the change lifecycle, not just at its beginning."}], "mcq": [{"question": "A target state primarily describes:", "choices": ["A vendor product", "A desired future organisational capability", "Only a database migration", "Current software defects"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Slide 5 distinguishes desired capability from a purchase idea.", "slides": "5"}, {"question": "Baseline architecture is:", "choices": ["An ideal future wish list", "The current state of business, data, applications and technology", "A marketing brochure", "A roadmap with dates only"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Baseline describes how the organisation operates now.", "slides": "6"}, {"question": "Which is a valid student-centred target outcome?", "choices": ["Buy any new portal", "One reliable enrolment status across systems", "Choose a new logo", "Replace all staff immediately"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "The lecture gives reliable shared status as a target example.", "slides": "5, 9"}, {"question": "What must be set before target design?", "choices": ["Only product branding", "Current performance and intended outcomes", "Only the vendor contract", "No measures are required"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Slide 8 says define success and establish baseline/target measures.", "slides": "8"}, {"question": "What is an architecture roadmap?", "choices": ["A list of technologies with no order", "A sequenced plan from baseline to target with dependencies", "A complete EA framework", "A department email"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Roadmaps organise initiatives, timing, dependencies and milestones.", "slides": "11"}, {"question": "Which roadmap dependency comes first?", "choices": ["Automate before defining the data", "Agree data ownership before automated exchange", "Release before security tests", "Disable support before launch"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Slide 12 specifically names this dependency.", "slides": "12"}, {"question": "Gap analysis compares:", "choices": ["Price with branding", "Baseline with target state", "DevOps with AI agents", "Two CSS styles"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Gap analysis identifies missing capabilities between states.", "slides": "16"}, {"question": "Which is primarily a governance gap?", "choices": ["No approval route for architecture exceptions", "Slow server response", "Duplicate student data", "Missing API"], "correct": 0, "explanation": "Slide 18 identifies no exception approval route as a governance gap.", "slides": "18"}, {"question": "What is a valid gap-analysis action for inconsistent status?", "choices": ["Ignore definitions", "Agree meaning and owner, design exchange and test reconciliation", "Buy hardware only", "Hide status from students"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Slide 17 shows these actions.", "slides": "17"}, {"question": "Architecture governance defines:", "choices": ["Only user-interface designs", "Principles, decision rights and review points", "Programming language syntax", "Guaranteed project success"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Governance clarifies how decisions are made and reviewed.", "slides": "19"}, {"question": "Which is an example architecture principle?", "choices": ["Every field has random owners", "Authoritative student data has a named owner", "Avoid documentation completely", "Interfaces must remain secret"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Slide 21 states this example.", "slides": "21"}, {"question": "An approved architecture exception should:", "choices": ["Remain secret", "Record the deviation and who accepted its risk", "Automatically override all security controls", "Avoid all review"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Exceptions require documented accountability.", "slides": "19–21"}, {"question": "TOGAF ADM is presented as:", "choices": ["A rigid never-revisited checklist", "Iterative and adaptable architecture development", "A scripting language", "A data storage format"], "correct": 1, "explanation": "Slide 23 emphasises iteration and tailoring.", "slides": "23"}, {"question": "Which belongs to the simplified ADM application?", "choices": ["Vision, Architecture, Solutions, Migration, Governance, Change", "Code only, Deploy only", "Buy, Advertise, Ignore", "Design UI, Count views"], "correct": 0, "explanation": "The lecture uses these six activities in the university case.", "slides": "25"}, {"question": "Which is NOT a lecture takeaway?", "choices": ["Roadmaps need dependencies", "Gap analysis creates actionable work", "Governance clarifies decisions", "Target state always means buying a new system"], "correct": 3, "explanation": "The lecture rejects product-first targets.", "slides": "30"}], "flashcards": [{"front": "What is the baseline architecture?", "back": "The organisation’s current business, data, applications and technology.", "slides": "6"}, {"front": "What is the target-state architecture?", "back": "The agreed future way of working, based on goals and constraints.", "slides": "6"}, {"front": "Why is “buy a new system” not a target?", "back": "It describes a solution, not an intended organisational capability.", "slides": "5"}, {"front": "What is the sample registration target?", "back": "One reliable enrolment status across systems.", "slides": "5"}, {"front": "Name four target-state EA layers.", "back": "Business, data, applications and technology.", "slides": "6–7"}, {"front": "Business target for registration?", "back": "Clear ownership and simpler steps.", "slides": "7"}, {"front": "Data target for registration?", "back": "Agreed definitions and authoritative student records.", "slides": "7"}, {"front": "Application target for registration?", "back": "Reliable exchange between portal and core systems.", "slides": "7"}, {"front": "Technology target for registration?", "back": "Resilient, secure, scalable and monitored service.", "slides": "7"}, {"front": "Give a student success measure.", "back": "Enrolment confirmation without repeated visits.", "slides": "8"}, {"front": "Give a staff success measure.", "back": "Fewer duplicate entries and manual corrections.", "slides": "8"}, {"front": "Give a management success measure.", "back": "Trustworthy, timely reporting.", "slides": "8"}, {"front": "Define an architecture roadmap.", "back": "A sequenced plan from baseline to target with dependencies and milestones.", "slides": "11"}, {"front": "Name three roadmap elements.", "back": "Initiatives, timing, dependencies, milestones and owners.", "slides": "11"}, {"front": "What precedes automated data exchange?", "back": "Agreement on data ownership and definitions.", "slides": "12"}, {"front": "What precedes migration?", "back": "Cleaning critical records.", "slides": "12"}, {"front": "What should be tested before peak registration?", "back": "Security, load and recovery.", "slides": "12"}, {"front": "What should be prepared before launch?", "back": "Staff and student support.", "slides": "12"}, {"front": "What does gap analysis compare?", "back": "Baseline to target, identifying missing capabilities.", "slides": "16"}, {"front": "Name five gap categories.", "back": "Business, data, application, technology and governance.", "slides": "18"}, {"front": "What is the case study’s data gap?", "back": "Missing agreed enrolment status, ownership and exchange rules.", "slides": "17"}, {"front": "What does governance establish?", "back": "Principles, decision rights and review points.", "slides": "19"}, {"front": "What three governance results appear?", "back": "Approve, revise or approve a documented exception.", "slides": "21"}, {"front": "What must a documented exception record?", "back": "The decision and who accepted its risk.", "slides": "19"}, {"front": "Example university data principle?", "back": "Authoritative student data has a named owner.", "slides": "21"}, {"front": "Example integration principle?", "back": "Use approved, documented interfaces.", "slides": "21"}, {"front": "What aspects are reviewed?", "back": "Business fit, data, integration, security, operations and cost.", "slides": "21"}, {"front": "What is TOGAF ADM?", "back": "TOGAF’s central, iterative architecture development method.", "slides": "23"}, {"front": "What must ADM be adapted to?", "back": "Scope, scale, risk and organisational capacity.", "slides": "23"}, {"front": "Name six simplified ADM steps.", "back": "Vision, Architecture, Solutions, Migration, Governance, Change.", "slides": "25"}, {"front": "What does Vision do in the case?", "back": "Defines reduced registration friction and more consistent records.", "slides": "25"}, {"front": "What does Migration do?", "back": "Plans stages, dependencies and cutover.", "slides": "25"}, {"front": "What does Change do?", "back": "Assesses new requirements and updates the roadmap.", "slides": "25"}, {"front": "Name five architecture work products.", "back": "Vision, baseline/target views, gaps, roadmap, governance record.", "slides": "26"}, {"front": "What is a product-first mistake?", "back": "Selecting a system before agreeing desired outcomes.", "slides": "27"}, {"front": "What is an unrealistic target mistake?", "back": "Treating the target as a perfect wish list.", "slides": "27"}, {"front": "What did the activity suggest as a roadmap?", "back": "Agree rules/baseline → pilot integration → phased rollout.", "slides": "29"}, {"front": "What measures are used in the activity answer?", "back": "Completion time, error rate, failed transactions and support volume.", "slides": "29"}, {"front": "What are the five lecture takeaways?", "back": "Target, roadmap, gaps, governance and adaptable ADM.", "slides": "30"}]};
const STRUCTURED=[{"section":"Part A · Structured","topic":"Baseline and target","slides":"5–7","scenario":"The university currently has inconsistent enrolment status across the student, course and finance systems.","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"Differentiate TWO architectural states needed for planning.","marks":4,"items":["Baseline architecture describes how the university currently works across the four EA layers.","Target-state architecture describes the agreed future operations and capabilities."],"model":"The baseline architecture describes how the university operates now, including current registration processes, data, applications and infrastructure. The target-state architecture describes the agreed future arrangement needed to meet the university’s goals, such as a reliable enrolment status shared across systems. Both views should cover the business, data, application and technology layers."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain THREE reasons both states are needed.","marks":6,"items":["The baseline gives evidence of current weaknesses and constraints.","The target clarifies the desired student and staff outcomes.","Comparing them reveals changes that can be sequenced and evaluated."],"model":"A useful target state begins with the outcome, not with a product purchase. First, the university should define a consistent registration process with clear responsibilities. Second, it should agree on an authoritative enrolment record and controlled exchange between the registration and finance systems. Third, it should provide secure, resilient hosting capable of handling peak registration demand. These requirements describe the capabilities the university needs, regardless of the particular vendor selected."}],"practiceSet":"Set 1","display":"Set 1 · Q1"},{"section":"Part A · Structured","topic":"Business outcomes","slides":"5, 8–9","scenario":"A university department defines its target as “buy a modern registration portal.”","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"Identify TWO reasons this is not a sufficient target state.","marks":4,"items":["Purchasing a product does not define a desired business capability.","A new portal does not automatically resolve inconsistent processes or data."],"model":"First, purchasing a product does not define a desired business capability. Second, a new portal does not automatically resolve inconsistent processes or data. Both are relevant when defining a feasible route from the current state to the desired future state."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain THREE measurable target outcomes for the university.","marks":6,"items":["Students should confirm enrolment without repeated visits.","Staff should perform fewer manual record corrections.","The service should remain reliable under peak registration demand."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, students should confirm enrolment without repeated visits. For the university, consistent enrolment information prevents contradictory statuses and repeated manual corrections. Second, staff should perform fewer manual record corrections. The organisation will not gain the intended benefit if people are unable or unwilling to use the new process. Third, the service should remain reliable under peak registration demand. The service must remain usable, or recover safely, when a component or connection fails. The roadmap should make university registration more reliable through coordinated process, data and system changes."}],"practiceSet":"Set 1","display":"Set 1 · Q2"},{"section":"Part A · Structured","topic":"Four-layer target","slides":"6–9","scenario":"The university wants one reliable course-registration status visible across departments.","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"Identify TWO proposed target-state changes in EA layers.","marks":4,"items":["Business: define one registration process and accountable process owners.","Data: agree consistent enrolment status and authoritative student records."],"model":"First, business: define one registration process and accountable process owners. Second, data: agree consistent enrolment status and authoritative student records. Both are relevant when defining a feasible route from the current state to the desired future state."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain THREE further implementation design requirements.","marks":6,"items":["Applications must exchange status through approved interfaces.","Technology must support secure, scalable and monitored service delivery.","Staff procedures and support arrangements must align with the target process."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, applications must exchange status through approved interfaces. The systems can then share necessary updates through controlled connections instead of manual re-entry. Second, technology must support secure, scalable and monitored service delivery. Recorded operational evidence makes problems visible and allows the team to investigate what happened. Third, staff procedures and support arrangements must align with the target process. The result should be demonstrated with comparable evidence, not simply asserted as a benefit. The roadmap should make university registration more reliable through coordinated process, data and system changes."}],"practiceSet":"Set 1","display":"Set 1 · Q3"},{"section":"Part A · Structured","topic":"Architecture roadmap","slides":"11–12","scenario":"A project team has described an ideal target state but has not planned how to reach it.","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"State TWO purposes of an architecture roadmap.","marks":4,"items":["It sequences initiatives needed to move from baseline to target.","It makes dependencies, owners and milestones visible."],"model":"First, it sequences initiatives needed to move from baseline to target. Second, it makes dependencies, owners and milestones visible. Both are relevant when defining a feasible route from the current state to the desired future state."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain THREE important dependencies in university registration.","marks":6,"items":["Agree data ownership before automating exchange.","Clean critical records before migration.","Test integration, security and recovery before full peak-period rollout."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, agree data ownership before automating exchange. A named source and clear meaning prevent different departments from treating conflicting copies as correct. Second, clean critical records before migration. Third, test integration, security and recovery before full peak-period rollout. The systems can then share necessary updates through controlled connections instead of manual re-entry."}],"practiceSet":"Set 1","display":"Set 1 · Q4"},{"section":"Part A · Structured","topic":"Roadmap stages","slides":"11–12, 29","scenario":"The university wants to modernise registration without interrupting the next enrolment period.","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"Propose TWO activities for an initial preparation stage.","marks":4,"items":["Map the current business process and capture baseline performance.","Assign data owners and agree shared definitions and priorities."],"model":"First, map the current business process and capture baseline performance. Clarifying who does what is necessary before automating the same activity in software. Second, assign data owners and agree shared definitions and priorities. A named source and clear meaning prevent different departments from treating conflicting copies as correct. Both are relevant when defining a feasible route from the current state to the desired future state."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain THREE later actions for controlled delivery.","marks":6,"items":["Pilot the integration using a limited set of services.","Test performance and recovery before a phased rollout.","Train staff and evaluate error rates and student experience after launch."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, pilot the integration using a limited set of services. The systems can then share necessary updates through controlled connections instead of manual re-entry. Second, test performance and recovery before a phased rollout. Doing prerequisite work first avoids costly rework and makes changes easier to test. Third, train staff and evaluate error rates and student experience after launch. A controlled introduction lets the team discover issues before they affect every user. The roadmap should make university registration more reliable through coordinated process, data and system changes."}],"practiceSet":"Set 1","display":"Set 1 · Q5"},{"section":"Part A · Structured","topic":"Gap analysis","slides":"16–18","scenario":"The student portal and finance system report different enrolment status for the same person.","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"Identify TWO relevant gaps.","marks":4,"items":["Data gap: enrolment status has inconsistent definitions or no trusted source.","Application gap: there is no dependable exchange between systems."],"model":"First, data gap: enrolment status has inconsistent definitions or no trusted source. Second, application gap: there is no dependable exchange between systems. Both are relevant when defining a feasible route from the current state to the desired future state."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain THREE parts of a useful gap-analysis record.","marks":6,"items":["Describe baseline evidence and the desired target capability.","State the impact, urgency and responsible owner.","Specify the corrective action and its dependency on agreeing data rules."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, describe baseline evidence and the desired target capability. The result should be demonstrated with comparable evidence, not simply asserted as a benefit. Second, state the impact, urgency and responsible owner. Third, specify the corrective action and its dependency on agreeing data rules. Doing prerequisite work first avoids costly rework and makes changes easier to test. The roadmap should make university registration more reliable through coordinated process, data and system changes."}],"practiceSet":"Set 2","display":"Set 2 · Q1"},{"section":"Part A · Structured","topic":"Beyond technology gaps","slides":"16–18","scenario":"A team solves software integration but staff are still unsure who may approve registration exceptions.","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"Identify TWO non-technical gap categories.","marks":4,"items":["Business gap: process responsibilities and approval rules remain unclear.","Governance gap: decision rights and exception approval routes have not been defined."],"model":"First, business gap: process responsibilities and approval rules remain unclear. Second, governance gap: decision rights and exception approval routes have not been defined. Both are relevant when defining a feasible route from the current state to the desired future state."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain THREE reasons technology fixes alone will not succeed.","marks":6,"items":["Different staff may continue to apply conflicting business rules.","Users may lose trust when decisions are inconsistent.","Unclear owners make future incidents and changes difficult to resolve."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, different staff may continue to apply conflicting business rules. Clarifying who does what is necessary before automating the same activity in software. Second, users may lose trust when decisions are inconsistent. Third, unclear owners make future incidents and changes difficult to resolve. The roadmap should make university registration more reliable through coordinated process, data and system changes."}],"practiceSet":"Set 2","display":"Set 2 · Q2"},{"section":"Part A · Structured","topic":"Governance","slides":"19–21","scenario":"A faculty requests a custom interface that conflicts with university-wide standards.","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"Identify TWO functions of architecture governance.","marks":4,"items":["Governance establishes principles and decision-making responsibilities.","It reviews proposed changes against business value, risk and architecture standards."],"model":"First, governance establishes principles and decision-making responsibilities. Second, it reviews proposed changes against business value, risk and architecture standards. Both are relevant when defining a feasible route from the current state to the desired future state."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain THREE possible review outcomes and their conditions.","marks":6,"items":["Approve when requirements and standards are satisfied.","Request revision when security, integration or business fit is inadequate.","Approve a documented exception only with identified risk acceptance and owner."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, approve when requirements and standards are satisfied. Clear decision authority means the team knows who can approve a change and accept its residual risks. Second, request revision when security, integration or business fit is inadequate. The systems can then share necessary updates through controlled connections instead of manual re-entry. Third, approve a documented exception only with identified risk acceptance and owner. The roadmap should make university registration more reliable through coordinated process, data and system changes."}],"practiceSet":"Set 2","display":"Set 2 · Q3"},{"section":"Part A · Structured","topic":"Architecture principles","slides":"19–21","scenario":"The university has no agreed rules for student-data ownership or how systems integrate.","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"State TWO architecture principles it could adopt.","marks":4,"items":["Each authoritative student-data item must have a named owner.","New integrations should use approved and documented interfaces."],"model":"First, each authoritative student-data item must have a named owner. Second, new integrations should use approved and documented interfaces. Both are relevant when defining a feasible route from the current state to the desired future state."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain THREE ways those principles influence project review.","marks":6,"items":["They enable reviewers to check data consistency and accountability.","They reduce unsafe ad hoc connections between applications.","They create evidence for approving, revising or documenting exceptions."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, they enable reviewers to check data consistency and accountability. Second, they reduce unsafe ad hoc connections between applications. Without this safeguard, a technically functional feature could still expose the organisation and its users to harm. Third, they create evidence for approving, revising or documenting exceptions. Clear decision authority means the team knows who can approve a change and accept its residual risks. The roadmap should make university registration more reliable through coordinated process, data and system changes."}],"practiceSet":"Set 2","display":"Set 2 · Q4"},{"section":"Part A · Structured","topic":"TOGAF ADM","slides":"23–25","scenario":"A campus project manager wants to apply TOGAF mechanically without considering available staff or project size.","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"Identify TWO ideas behind TOGAF ADM.","marks":4,"items":["ADM is a structured method for architecture development.","ADM is iterative, so teams can revisit decisions as new evidence appears."],"model":"First, aDM is a structured method for architecture development. Second, aDM is iterative, so teams can revisit decisions as new evidence appears. Both are relevant when defining a feasible route from the current state to the desired future state."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain THREE ways ADM can be used appropriately in the campus case.","marks":6,"items":["Begin with a vision and business outcomes for registration.","Develop architecture and possible solutions before migration.","Adapt reviews and governance to the university’s scope, risk and capacity."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, begin with a vision and business outcomes for registration. The result should be demonstrated with comparable evidence, not simply asserted as a benefit. Second, develop architecture and possible solutions before migration. The choice should support the wider system and organisational goals rather than solve only one isolated technical problem. Third, adapt reviews and governance to the university’s scope, risk and capacity. Clear decision authority means the team knows who can approve a change and accept its residual risks."}],"practiceSet":"Set 2","display":"Set 2 · Q5"},{"section":"Part A · Structured","topic":"ADM application","slides":"23–26","scenario":"The university wants to introduce a connected course-registration service.","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"Identify TWO early activities in a simplified ADM.","marks":4,"items":["Vision: agree the goal of reducing registration friction.","Architecture: define business, data, application and platform requirements."],"model":"First, the next step is vision: agree the goal of reducing registration friction. Second, the next step is architecture: define business, data, application and platform requirements. Both are relevant when defining a feasible route from the current state to the desired future state."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain THREE later activities of the simplified approach.","marks":6,"items":["Solutions: compare implementation options and pilot choices.","Migration: plan stages and dependencies for cutover.","Governance and change: verify compliance and update architecture when needs change."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, solutions: compare implementation options and pilot choices. A controlled introduction lets the team discover issues before they affect every user. Second, the next step is migration: plan stages and dependencies for cutover. Doing prerequisite work first avoids costly rework and makes changes easier to test. Third, governance and change: verify compliance and update architecture when needs change. Clear decision authority means the team knows who can approve a change and accept its residual risks. The roadmap should make university registration more reliable through coordinated process, data and system changes."}],"practiceSet":"Set 3","display":"Set 3 · Q1"},{"section":"Part A · Structured","topic":"Work products","slides":"16–21, 26","scenario":"The rector asks the EA team to present tangible outputs from the planning exercise.","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"Identify TWO essential architecture work products.","marks":4,"items":["Architecture vision records the scope, problem, stakeholders and outcomes.","Baseline and target views document current and intended architecture."],"model":"First, architecture vision records the scope, problem, stakeholders and outcomes. Second, baseline and target views document current and intended architecture. Both are relevant when defining a feasible route from the current state to the desired future state."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain THREE further work products and their uses.","marks":6,"items":["Gap analysis identifies missing capabilities and required actions.","Roadmap sequences initiatives, owners and milestones.","Governance record documents approvals, exceptions and follow-up decisions."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, gap analysis identifies missing capabilities and required actions. Second, roadmap sequences initiatives, owners and milestones. Doing prerequisite work first avoids costly rework and makes changes easier to test. Third, governance record documents approvals, exceptions and follow-up decisions. Clear decision authority means the team knows who can approve a change and accept its residual risks."}],"practiceSet":"Set 3","display":"Set 3 · Q2"},{"section":"Part A · Structured","topic":"Mistakes","slides":"27","scenario":"The project buys a product first, creates a roadmap without dependencies and only asks for governance sign-off at the end.","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"Identify TWO mistakes made by the team.","marks":4,"items":["It selected a product before agreeing the future business capability.","The roadmap ignores work dependencies and accountable owners."],"model":"First, it selected a product before agreeing the future business capability. Second, the roadmap ignores work dependencies and accountable owners. Both are relevant when defining a feasible route from the current state to the desired future state."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain THREE corrections consistent with the lecture.","marks":6,"items":["Define target outcomes and constraints before vendor selection.","Order changes so ownership, data and integration readiness come first.","Use governance reviews throughout rather than as final paperwork."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, define target outcomes and constraints before vendor selection. The result should be demonstrated with comparable evidence, not simply asserted as a benefit. Second, order changes so ownership, data and integration readiness come first. The systems can then share necessary updates through controlled connections instead of manual re-entry. Third, use governance reviews throughout rather than as final paperwork. Clear decision authority means the team knows who can approve a change and accept its residual risks. The decision, its owner and any accepted exception should be recorded and reviewed."}],"practiceSet":"Set 3","display":"Set 3 · Q3"},{"section":"Part A · Structured","topic":"Success measures","slides":"8, 20, 29","scenario":"After a pilot, the student portal seems quicker, but management cannot establish whether outcomes improved.","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"Identify TWO possible baseline indicators.","marks":4,"items":["Average time required to complete registration.","Number of failed transactions or manual corrections."],"model":"First, average time required to complete registration. Second, number of failed transactions or manual corrections. Both are relevant when defining a feasible route from the current state to the desired future state."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain THREE ways to assess value after the pilot.","marks":6,"items":["Compare measured results against the same pre-implementation definitions.","Review student and staff experience rather than performance alone.","Decide whether further rollout is justified by evidence and risk."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, compare measured results against the same pre-implementation definitions. The result should be demonstrated with comparable evidence, not simply asserted as a benefit. Second, review student and staff experience rather than performance alone. The organisation will not gain the intended benefit if people are unable or unwilling to use the new process. Third, decide whether further rollout is justified by evidence and risk. A controlled introduction lets the team discover issues before they affect every user. The roadmap should make university registration more reliable through coordinated process, data and system changes."}],"practiceSet":"Set 3","display":"Set 3 · Q4"},{"section":"Part A · Structured","topic":"End-to-end scenario","slides":"5–9, 16–29","scenario":"A new university learning platform is proposed, but the student-record and finance interfaces are not tested.","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"Identify TWO high-priority risks for the target state.","marks":4,"items":["Enrolment and fee status may remain inconsistent between applications.","Untested integrations may fail during peak registration."],"model":"First, enrolment and fee status may remain inconsistent between applications. Second, untested integrations may fail during peak registration. Both are relevant when defining a feasible route from the current state to the desired future state."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain THREE actions before the institution approves rollout.","marks":6,"items":["Resolve authoritative data definitions and interface contracts.","Complete performance, security and recovery tests.","Assign architecture governance owners to review risks and rollout dependencies."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, resolve authoritative data definitions and interface contracts. A named source and clear meaning prevent different departments from treating conflicting copies as correct. Second, complete performance, security and recovery tests. Without this safeguard, a technically functional feature could still expose the organisation and its users to harm. Third, assign architecture governance owners to review risks and rollout dependencies. Doing prerequisite work first avoids costly rework and makes changes easier to test. The roadmap should make university registration more reliable through coordinated process, data and system changes."}],"practiceSet":"Set 3","display":"Set 3 · Q5"},{"section":"Part B · Case Study","topic":"University EA transformation","slides":"5–29","scenario":"A university operates a fragmented course-registration process. Students repeat data entry, staff reconcile records manually and the portal fails at peak hours. The CIO requests a target state and change plan.","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"Identify FIVE elements of the desired target state.","marks":5,"items":["One clearer student registration process.","Named owners for critical student and enrolment data.","Approved integration between portal, finance and academic systems.","Secure resilient infrastructure under high demand.","Staff support and reliable status reporting."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, one clearer student registration process. Second, named owners for critical student and enrolment data. Third, approved integration between portal, finance and academic systems. Fourth, secure resilient infrastructure under high demand. Fifth, staff support and reliable status reporting."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain FIVE priority gaps with corresponding actions.","marks":10,"items":["Unclear process ownership: assign accountable offices.","Inconsistent status definitions: agree shared data rules.","Missing interface contracts: design and test integration.","Weak monitoring and recovery: improve operations and resilience.","No exception approval process: establish governance decision rights."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, unclear process ownership: assign accountable offices. Clarifying who does what is necessary before automating the same activity in software. Second, inconsistent status definitions: agree shared data rules. Third, missing interface contracts: design and test integration. The systems can then share necessary updates through controlled connections instead of manual re-entry. Fourth, weak monitoring and recovery: improve operations and resilience. Recorded operational evidence makes problems visible and allows the team to investigate what happened. Fifth, no exception approval process: establish governance decision rights. Clear decision authority means the team knows who can approve a change and accept its residual risks. The roadmap should make university registration more reliable through coordinated process, data and system changes."},{"letter":"c","question":"Propose FIVE roadmap and governance decisions.","marks":10,"items":["Stage 1: map baseline, agree owners, define KPIs.","Stage 2: clean data and pilot controlled integration.","Stage 3: test and roll out in phases with training.","Govern student-data definitions and integration/security reviews.","Compare outcomes against baseline and revise architecture when necessary."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, stage 1: map baseline, agree owners, define KPIs. The result should be demonstrated with comparable evidence, not simply asserted as a benefit. Second, stage 2: clean data and pilot controlled integration. The systems can then share necessary updates through controlled connections instead of manual re-entry. Third, stage 3: test and roll out in phases with training. Doing prerequisite work first avoids costly rework and makes changes easier to test. Fourth, govern student-data definitions and integration/security reviews. A named source and clear meaning prevent different departments from treating conflicting copies as correct. Fifth, compare outcomes against baseline and revise architecture when necessary. The choice should support the wider system and organisational goals rather than solve only one isolated technical problem. The roadmap should make university registration more reliable through coordinated process, data and system changes."}],"practiceSet":"Case studies","display":"Case Study 1"},{"section":"Part B · Case Study","topic":"Architecture governance under pressure","slides":"16–27","scenario":"The registration team is under deadline pressure. A vendor wants to launch despite inconsistent student records, no recovery test and an unreviewed integration exception.","parts":[{"letter":"a","question":"Identify FIVE weaknesses of the current plan.","marks":5,"items":["Inconsistent records prevent reliable status exchange.","Unclear data ownership leaves corrections unaccountable.","Unreviewed integration creates security and compatibility risk.","Missing recovery testing threatens availability.","Deadline pressure is overriding governed decision-making."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, inconsistent records prevent reliable status exchange. Second, unclear data ownership leaves corrections unaccountable. Third, unreviewed integration creates security and compatibility risk. Fourth, missing recovery testing threatens availability. Fifth, deadline pressure is overriding governed decision-making."},{"letter":"b","question":"Explain FIVE architecture-governance responses.","marks":10,"items":["Require a named business and technical owner for critical decisions.","Apply agreed principles to data and interface design.","Review business fit, cost, security and operational risks.","Document any approved exception and accepted risk.","Defer unsafe release until important controls are demonstrated."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, require a named business and technical owner for critical decisions. Second, apply agreed principles to data and interface design. The systems can then share necessary updates through controlled connections instead of manual re-entry. Third, review business fit, cost, security and operational risks. Without this safeguard, a technically functional feature could still expose the organisation and its users to harm. Fourth, document any approved exception and accepted risk. Clear decision authority means the team knows who can approve a change and accept its residual risks. Fifth, defer unsafe release until important controls are demonstrated. The roadmap should make university registration more reliable through coordinated process, data and system changes."},{"letter":"c","question":"Justify FIVE changes to the roadmap and review process.","marks":10,"items":["Prioritise data definitions and cleaning before full migration.","Integrate through a limited pilot with measurable exit criteria.","Test peak-load behaviour and recovery before cutover.","Prepare users and support staff for the new workflows.","Maintain continuous review, feedback and updates after deployment."],"model":"The change should be managed against the agreed target state and its dependencies. First, prioritise data definitions and cleaning before full migration. A named source and clear meaning prevent different departments from treating conflicting copies as correct. Second, integrate through a limited pilot with measurable exit criteria. A controlled introduction lets the team discover issues before they affect every user. Third, test peak-load behaviour and recovery before cutover. The service must remain usable, or recover safely, when a component or connection fails. Fourth, prepare users and support staff for the new workflows. Clarifying who does what is necessary before automating the same activity in software. Fifth, maintain continuous review, feedback and updates after deployment. Linking development to real operating conditions helps teams improve the service after each release. The roadmap should make university registration more reliable through coordinated process, data and system changes."}],"practiceSet":"Case studies","display":"Case Study 2"}];
